   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general obligations law, in relation to genetically\nmodified organisms",
    "basePrintNo": "S4206",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4206-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §9-107, Gen Ob L",
    "lawSection": "General Obligations Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4206\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general obligations law, in\nrelation to genetically modified organisms\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow for an affirmative defense against\nany liability if the party can show that he or she did not knowingly and\nintentionally introduce the genetically engineered or genetically modi-\nfied organisms into his or her plants or seeds.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the general obligations law by adding a new\nsection 9-107 to provide an affirmative defense against any liability if\nthe party who is sued for damages of a patent, trademark, or other\nintellectual property rights shows that he or she did not knowingly and\nintentionally introduce the genetically engineered or genetically modi-\nfied organisms into his or her plants or seeds or onto his or her prop-\nerty and he or she did not knowingly gain from the distinctive traits\ndue to genetic modification or genetic engineering.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill seeks to help farmers by providing them with an affirmative\ndefense if they are sued for planting genetically modified organisms or\ngenetically engineered plants without purchasing the rights to do so.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017/18: A600 passed Assembly; S523 (Young) referred to judici- ary;\n2015/16: A205 Passed Assembly, S.3085 (Young) referred to judi- ciary;\n2013/14: A5323 Passed Assembly; S4315 (Young) referred to judi- ciary;\n2011/12: A.2111/S.785: Passed Assembly; 5758 (Young) referred to judi-\nciary\n2009/10: A.2373/S.2483: Passed Assembly; S2483 (Young) referred to judi-\nciary\n2007/08: A.1403/S.4951: Passed Assembly; 54951 (Young) referred to judi-\nciary\n2005/06: A.1468: Passed Assembly;\n2003/04: A.1911;\n2001/02: A.11674\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
